1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Thundering power, deafening noise, brute force that crushes anything in its path.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
They're big, they're bad, they're loud.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:15,000
Now, Monster Trucks on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:29,001
They rage like mythic beasts across the land.

5
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:36,940
With their formidable powers, they have only each other for competition.

6
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:41,240
The big boys with the big toys are bags.

7
00:00:41,480 --> 00:00:43,940
The Milestone Motorsports Extravaganza.

8
00:00:43,941 --> 00:00:44,641
Hands off.

9
00:00:44,740 --> 00:00:50,800
We challenge Shums Thursday, Friday and Saturday, June 3rd, 4th and 5th at Devil's Bowl Speedway and Musquey.

10
00:00:53,060 --> 00:00:54,400
Bakersfield, California.

11
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:58,240
A city surrounded by farms where pickup trucks abound.

12
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:02,840
Excited fans are gathered here to see a different kind of pickup truck.

13
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:08,060
One that seems dreamlike.

14
00:01:08,580 --> 00:01:10,680
The ultimate expression of power on wheels.

15
00:01:10,681 --> 00:01:14,380
The name of the beast is the monster truck.

16
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:18,780
And when these titans clash, anything can happen.

17
00:01:27,120 --> 00:01:28,840
People come to see wrecks.

18
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:32,500
What we're doing is almost like a wreck every time we go out and race,

19
00:01:32,501 --> 00:01:34,420
whether we get in trouble or not,

20
00:01:34,540 --> 00:01:36,300
where there's going to be some kind of violent riot.

21
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:39,540
The big challenge is these things are 10,000-pound beasts,

22
00:01:39,541 --> 00:01:41,340
and so they don't turn real well.

23
00:01:44,020 --> 00:01:45,620
The noise is deafening.

24
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:47,500
The stunts are unbelievable.

25
00:01:48,280 --> 00:01:49,740
The wrecks are incredible.

26
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:52,460
It's very, very loud,

27
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:54,160
which is something that doesn't come across,

28
00:01:54,260 --> 00:01:57,220
especially when it's in an indoor domed stadium

29
00:01:57,221 --> 00:01:59,380
where a lot of these events take place.

30
00:01:59,480 --> 00:02:01,040
So there's mud flying.

31
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:03,980
People are hitting it faced with dirt.

32
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:04,780
They get dirty.

33
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:07,760
They love the roar of the engines, the power.

34
00:02:07,761 --> 00:02:15,973
I think more than anything, people love monster trucks because they wish they were in one when they were in a traffic jam at 5 o'clock in one of the major cities in

35
00:02:15,974 --> 00:02:20,080
America so that they could just drive over the cars in front of them and get home.

36
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:26,820
Monster truck racing is one of the fastest growing motorsports in the world.

37
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:33,880
From small town dirt tracks to big city indoor stadiums, fans can't get enough of these motorized mammoths.

38
00:02:34,420 --> 00:02:36,420
Monster truck is an overgrown pickup truck.

39
00:02:36,421 --> 00:02:39,340
A pickup truck might weigh 3,000 or 4,000 pounds.

40
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:42,240
A monster truck weighs 5 tons, 10,000 pounds.

41
00:02:45,740 --> 00:02:49,420
When you're in the air over, say, 6, 7 cars,

42
00:02:49,700 --> 00:02:52,200
and you're 14 feet in the air,

43
00:02:52,360 --> 00:02:53,440
that gives you enough time.

44
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:54,780
You get to actually look around.

45
00:02:55,860 --> 00:02:57,900
And knowing what you have,

46
00:02:58,100 --> 00:03:00,420
the mass of machinery you have in the air,

47
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:03,320
it's an ego booster, let's just say that.

48
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:12,580
No one has flown higher or farther in a monster truck than Dan Runty, driver of Bigfoot 14.

49
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:18,740
At a Tennessee air show in 1999, he attempted a jump over a 727 jetliner.

50
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:24,340
To jump this high and this far, the takeoff ramp has to be much steeper,

51
00:03:24,680 --> 00:03:28,340
and the truck's speed much higher than Dan Runty is used to in normal racing.

52
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:34,260
Passing over, I had a chance to glance out the window and see where the tail section of the airplane was,

53
00:03:34,261 --> 00:03:38,780
And that's when I realized, wow, this is like way up in the air.

54
00:03:39,020 --> 00:03:42,020
If you take a 10,000-pound truck and throw it that high in the air,

55
00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:44,000
you don't know what's going to happen when it comes down.

56
00:03:44,280 --> 00:03:45,240
It was pretty hairy.

57
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:48,260
It hit hard, and on the rebound bounce,

58
00:03:48,360 --> 00:03:53,580
it actually bounced 100 to 110 foot before it touched down the second time.

59
00:03:54,740 --> 00:03:58,380
And when it was over, Dan Runty drove off with a new record.

60
00:03:58,780 --> 00:04:02,560
He had jumped 202 feet, 50 feet more than the old record.

61
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:10,640
Even in a regular monster truck race, coming down from a jump can be punishing on the driver.

62
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:15,280
Your first impact, it's a stopping impact.

63
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:20,180
As the front end comes up, then, so your neck goes forward, your head goes forward.

64
00:04:20,460 --> 00:04:26,420
And then from about here to probably about three inches under your shoulders, it's taken all the stretch.

65
00:04:27,580 --> 00:04:31,900
And so it, jacuzzis are driver's best friends.

66
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:39,700
In the past, drivers have broken their backs when they were slammed into their seats by the impact of a hard landing.

67
00:04:41,080 --> 00:04:45,400
But today's monster trucks are much safer, and driver injuries are rare,

68
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:48,940
thanks mainly to the technological innovations of one man.

69
00:04:49,960 --> 00:04:53,720
Bob Chandler and his wife Marilyn created the very first monster truck.

70
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:56,420
Yeah. Oh, I remember when I used to go in the mud all the time.

71
00:04:56,421 --> 00:04:59,520
It would take you two days to clean all the mud out of the truck. It was horrible.

72
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:01,600
Remember when I used to bring it home and clean it in the driveway?

73
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:04,140
Yes, and we'd have a pile of mud in the driveway everybody liked.

74
00:05:04,260 --> 00:05:10,460
The monster truck was born in the mid-70s when the Chandlers opened up a four-wheel drive parts business.

75
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:16,040
To help promote it, Bob decided to customize his own four-wheel drive Ford pickup.

76
00:05:16,820 --> 00:05:20,000
The more he changed the truck, the more attention it received.

77
00:05:20,620 --> 00:05:26,340
And he gradually just made this truck bigger and stronger so that there was really no other vehicle like ours.

78
00:05:27,980 --> 00:05:33,160
Because Bob Chandler was known for having a heavy foot on the gas, his nickname was Bigfoot.

79
00:05:33,161 --> 00:05:35,480
And that's the name he put on his truck.

80
00:05:36,700 --> 00:05:39,640
The average monster truck race lasts 5 to 10 seconds,

81
00:05:39,820 --> 00:05:42,880
during which about 6 gallons of methanol fuel are consumed.

82
00:05:44,460 --> 00:05:46,940
Monster Trucks will return on Modern Marvels.

83
00:05:51,060 --> 00:05:54,080
Monster Truck mania has proved to be highly contagious,

84
00:05:54,660 --> 00:05:57,020
and Americans aren't the only ones who are susceptible.

85
00:05:58,360 --> 00:06:01,200
They've exported Bigfoot around the world.

86
00:06:01,201 --> 00:06:04,920
They're big feet, if I may, doing performances.

87
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:07,860
And people look at this and they say, this is American.

88
00:06:08,220 --> 00:06:12,900
It's the big engine, it's the big tires, it's the big roar, it's the gas guzzlers.

89
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:15,380
They seem to think that this is Americana.

90
00:06:17,060 --> 00:06:22,720
Although monster trucks are a relatively new form of motorsport, their roots go back a long way.

91
00:06:22,721 --> 00:06:31,440
In the late 1800s, farmers sometimes held contests to see whose horse could pull the most weight.

92
00:06:31,860 --> 00:06:36,240
But in the early 1900s, tractors began to replace horses on the farm.

93
00:06:36,980 --> 00:06:42,840
And by the 1930s, tractor pull tournaments known as tug pulls were being held throughout the Midwest.

94
00:06:44,200 --> 00:06:49,060
The tractors pulled a sled of steel that was gradually loaded with more and more weight.

95
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:59,720
Early monster trucks often appeared at shows where the featured attraction was a tractor pull contest.

96
00:07:07,720 --> 00:07:15,720
Today's tractor pullers can be even more powerful and more expensive than monster trucks since they often have multiple engines.

97
00:07:15,721 --> 00:07:26,400
Like monster trucks, the tractors have huge tires, but only on the rear axle where they

98
00:07:26,401 --> 00:07:28,200
need the traction.

99
00:07:28,201 --> 00:07:33,420
Their front tires are much smaller.

100
00:07:33,421 --> 00:07:38,720
The tires monster trucks use today, called flotation tires, are manufactured for agricultural

101
00:07:38,721 --> 00:07:39,720
use.

102
00:07:39,721 --> 00:07:44,820
Machines like this spreader have used the large, low-pressure tires for decades to gain solid

103
00:07:44,821 --> 00:07:46,566
footing in wet, muddy fields.

104
00:07:46,567 --> 00:07:50,720
Many monster truck owners shave down the thick rubber tread to reduce

105
00:07:50,721 --> 00:07:56,472
weight. This can lighten each tire by as much as 200 pounds.

106
00:07:56,473 --> 00:07:59,540
Monster truck tires need to flex

107
00:07:59,541 --> 00:08:02,000
because the tire is part of the suspension.

108
00:08:02,001 --> 00:08:05,260
That's why the low-pressure agricultural tires are ideal.

109
00:08:06,660 --> 00:08:12,760
The huge tires on earth movers, like the 785 Caterpillar mining truck, would never work on

110
00:08:12,761 --> 00:08:20,140
monster trucks they're too heavy and too rigid for years the military has relied

111
00:08:20,141 --> 00:08:25,120
on its own brand of monster truck on the battlefield today one of the most rugged

112
00:08:25,121 --> 00:08:30,760
and versatile is the HEMTT made by the Oshkosh Truck Corporation in Wisconsin

113
00:08:30,761 --> 00:08:36,160
the letters stand for heavy expanded mobility tactical truck it's basically a

114
00:08:36,161 --> 00:08:42,340
recovery vehicle to recover stub mired or broke down vehicles it's good for all

115
00:08:42,341 --> 00:08:44,080
all types of terrain and all types of weather.

116
00:08:44,740 --> 00:08:47,640
I've drove one of these for many years and never got one stubbed.

117
00:08:48,880 --> 00:08:54,620
The HEMTT has eight-wheel drive, giving it the traction to go just about anywhere.

118
00:08:55,460 --> 00:08:59,580
It steers with both of its front axles, allowing more stability in turns.

119
00:09:00,840 --> 00:09:04,020
Perhaps the best-known military off-road truck is the Hummer.

120
00:09:04,740 --> 00:09:08,940
Not only is it at home on the battlefield, it's also great for picking up groceries.

121
00:09:09,580 --> 00:09:12,360
I've seen the Hummer do some pretty amazing climbing.

122
00:09:13,020 --> 00:09:15,740
I've seen it rip through sand in the Mojave Desert.

123
00:09:16,560 --> 00:09:20,420
Going through your basic mud any place you need to be, I think it does great.

124
00:09:22,520 --> 00:09:25,820
Military trucks, tractor pullers, and agricultural equipment

125
00:09:25,821 --> 00:09:28,500
were some of the disparate elements that fused together

126
00:09:28,501 --> 00:09:31,060
when Bob Chandler created the monster truck.

127
00:09:32,340 --> 00:09:35,720
And in 1981, in a deserted Missouri cornfield,

128
00:09:36,340 --> 00:09:38,360
Bob Chandler was making history again.

129
00:09:38,940 --> 00:09:44,660
Just for fun, Chandler decided to drive Bigfoot on top of some old cars.

130
00:09:45,340 --> 00:09:48,320
I just drove up on top and drove right over him. It was really easy.

131
00:09:49,100 --> 00:09:51,820
And we went around a second time and pulled up and stopped on top of him.

132
00:09:52,140 --> 00:09:55,140
And we videotaped this. It was something different, you know.

133
00:09:55,260 --> 00:09:56,360
I just wanted to see if it was going to work.

134
00:09:57,700 --> 00:10:02,460
Later, when Bob Chandler showed the tape to his wife, Marilyn, she didn't like what she saw.

135
00:10:03,100 --> 00:10:06,300
I thought it was really destructive, and I wasn't that crazy about it.

136
00:10:06,301 --> 00:10:10,560
So I think that Bob and I both felt that way for a long time, that we didn't like it.

137
00:10:10,920 --> 00:10:11,880
A promoter saw it.

138
00:10:12,740 --> 00:10:15,080
A promoter wanted me to do it in front of the crowd, and I wouldn't do it.

139
00:10:15,280 --> 00:10:18,500
I said, it's too destructive. It's just not the image that we wanted, you know.

140
00:10:19,880 --> 00:10:21,240
Finally, he convinced me to try it.

141
00:10:21,360 --> 00:10:25,520
It never dawned on us that this would really lead to any kind of phenomenon that would take over.

142
00:10:26,420 --> 00:10:33,040
But the Chandlers discovered to their amazement that stadium crowds went wild when Bigfoot crushed cars.

143
00:10:33,041 --> 00:10:36,760
at one of Bigfoot's very first arena car crushes

144
00:10:36,761 --> 00:10:39,240
at the Pontiac Silverdome in 1983

145
00:10:39,241 --> 00:10:43,040
thousands of fans jumped the railings and surrounded the truck

146
00:10:43,041 --> 00:10:46,320
our son who was very young was in the truck with Bob

147
00:10:46,321 --> 00:10:50,520
and he drove up on the cars and we saw a million flashballs go off

148
00:10:50,521 --> 00:10:52,000
people started yelling and screaming

149
00:10:52,001 --> 00:10:54,540
and then they all just piled into the field

150
00:10:54,541 --> 00:10:56,400
I mean jumped right over the barricades and everything

151
00:10:56,401 --> 00:10:58,500
and came down to the field which of course stopped the show

152
00:10:59,440 --> 00:11:03,460
The phenomenon caught on and became bigger than Bigfoot.

153
00:11:04,140 --> 00:11:08,520
By the mid-80s, there were several other monster trucks around to compete with Bob Chandler's.

154
00:11:09,340 --> 00:11:13,800
The new trucks had names like Barefoot, Carolina Crusher, and Gravedigger.

155
00:11:14,840 --> 00:11:19,500
And over the next decade, monster truck design evolved through three major stages.

156
00:11:20,600 --> 00:11:26,980
Stage one was simply a regular four-wheel drive pickup truck that was raised and had big tires mounted on it.

157
00:11:28,500 --> 00:11:35,620
In stage two, the pickup trucks got heavier frames, axles, and wheels, which were taken from larger trucks.

158
00:11:36,480 --> 00:11:42,680
This made the monster pickups stronger, but it soon became apparent their suspensions couldn't handle the extra weight.

159
00:11:45,320 --> 00:11:50,880
We started racing probably seven or eight years ago, and we found out that that wouldn't work.

160
00:11:50,980 --> 00:11:53,880
There was people getting hurt because they were too rough, there was no suspension on them.

161
00:11:53,881 --> 00:11:59,080
It was obvious the monster truck world was ready for stage three.

162
00:11:59,720 --> 00:12:06,500
So Bob Chandler went back to the drawing board, creating a much lighter and safer design that would become the industry standard.

163
00:12:07,260 --> 00:12:11,200
I studied dune buggies. I studied drag vehicles. I studied off-road vehicles.

164
00:12:11,340 --> 00:12:15,800
I studied truck and tractor pulling vehicles, things that I've seen over the years and kind of combined whole works.

165
00:12:15,801 --> 00:12:21,960
I designed on a computer a tube chassis, a frame, and put a lot of suspension travel on it.

166
00:12:21,961 --> 00:12:24,320
Before we were running maybe six to eight inches of suspension,

167
00:12:24,720 --> 00:12:28,280
and now we're up to three foot, sometimes three and a half foot of suspension travel.

168
00:12:29,080 --> 00:12:32,940
As monster trucks have evolved, so have the skills of the drivers.

169
00:12:33,580 --> 00:12:36,620
Bob Chandler has doubts whether he could drive competitively today.

170
00:12:37,640 --> 00:12:39,700
He gave up driving Bigfoot years ago

171
00:12:39,701 --> 00:12:43,360
after watching one of his younger drivers recover from a difficult jump.

172
00:12:45,280 --> 00:12:47,980
And he'd come over the last set of cars on his nose,

173
00:12:48,200 --> 00:12:50,140
and he had a wall in front of him,

174
00:12:50,141 --> 00:12:53,140
And he had to power the truck to get it down, and he hit the brakes and went back up.

175
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:57,060
He had to power it again to get it down again and hit the brakes, and he slid sideways and just kissed the wall.

176
00:13:01,600 --> 00:13:04,500
Now, I know I could drive the truck like he does around the courses,

177
00:13:05,140 --> 00:13:11,780
but to be able to do all this spontaneously, to get the brakes, get the gas, get the brakes, turn, everything else at one time, I don't think I could do it.

178
00:13:14,160 --> 00:13:20,080
In 1983, Bob Chandler made a dramatic new discovery about the capabilities of Monster Truck.

179
00:13:20,140 --> 00:13:24,600
Like many scientific discoveries, it happened by accident.

180
00:13:26,460 --> 00:13:30,660
He was trying to get Bigfoot's rear tires submerged in a lake for a photo shoot.

181
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:33,420
But found it was impossible.

182
00:13:34,120 --> 00:13:37,860
Because not only do monster trucks fly, they also float.

183
00:13:38,780 --> 00:13:43,500
The high volume of air in the tires provides enough buoyancy to keep the heavy truck from sinking.

184
00:13:44,740 --> 00:13:48,960
That led to a race with a paddle wheeler down the Chattahoochee River in 1985.

185
00:13:49,780 --> 00:13:54,760
Bigfoot lost, so Bob Chandler then challenged the paddle wheeler's captain to a race down Main Street.

186
00:13:55,620 --> 00:13:56,760
The captain declined.

187
00:13:59,960 --> 00:14:05,800
The average monster truck costs about $150,000 and takes from 3 to 12 months to build.

188
00:14:06,520 --> 00:14:11,800
The cost of maintaining and transporting it during a year of racing can be as high as $120,000.

189
00:14:12,760 --> 00:14:15,460
Monster Trucks will return on Modern Marvels.

190
00:14:15,461 --> 00:14:24,040
The modern monster truck is a triumph of technology, with more moving parts than any other racing vehicle.

191
00:14:26,020 --> 00:14:34,700
The power plants of most monster trucks are based on conventional Ford, Chevrolet, or Chrysler blocks, but they don't stay conventional for long.

192
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:41,400
The blocks are bored out and supercharged, with a maximum displacement of 575 cubic inches.

193
00:14:42,100 --> 00:14:44,960
Power varies depending on how the truck is set up and tuned.

194
00:14:45,460 --> 00:14:48,300
but it can range as high as 1,700 horsepower.

195
00:14:52,600 --> 00:14:56,520
These days, most monster trucks have their engines mounted behind the driver

196
00:14:56,521 --> 00:14:58,300
in a mid-engine configuration.

197
00:14:59,080 --> 00:15:02,080
This gives the truck much better balance than a front-mounted engine.

198
00:15:05,020 --> 00:15:08,360
From a distance, they appear to have standard pickup truck bodies,

199
00:15:08,820 --> 00:15:10,080
but it's just an illusion.

200
00:15:10,740 --> 00:15:14,360
The body is actually a fiberglass shell molded in the shape of a truck.

201
00:15:15,460 --> 00:15:19,620
The headlights and grille may look real from the stadium seats, but they're just painted on.

202
00:15:21,420 --> 00:15:26,380
The suspension of the modern monster truck is an innovation developed by the Bigfoot team.

203
00:15:28,040 --> 00:15:32,240
These specially constructed shock absorbers, which cost as much as $1,000 each,

204
00:15:32,780 --> 00:15:35,820
can handle the impact of landing from a 200-foot jump.

205
00:15:39,440 --> 00:15:44,180
Unlike a regular truck, the monsters have both front and rear wheel steering.

206
00:15:44,940 --> 00:15:49,580
The rear wheel steering is activated by a switch that instantly turns the wheels

207
00:15:49,581 --> 00:15:52,740
and then automatically centers them after the turn is made.

208
00:15:53,480 --> 00:15:56,640
This allows the trucks to go around corners faster.

209
00:15:58,140 --> 00:16:02,440
One of the biggest problems with early monster trucks was that the axles broke

210
00:16:02,441 --> 00:16:05,420
because of the strain of driving the huge wheels and tires.

211
00:16:06,420 --> 00:16:09,980
To make that big tire work, you couldn't get an axle strong enough.

212
00:16:10,240 --> 00:16:12,740
For our size engine, we'd break axles all the time.

213
00:16:12,741 --> 00:16:14,380
we break transmission, we break everything else.

214
00:16:14,740 --> 00:16:17,080
So off the big equipment we pull a planetary.

215
00:16:17,500 --> 00:16:20,940
The planetary is nothing more than a gear reduction at the wheel.

216
00:16:21,500 --> 00:16:26,140
The so-called planetary gear makes it easier for the axle to turn the huge wheels.

217
00:16:27,140 --> 00:16:31,320
It consists of a central sun gear surrounded by three planet gears,

218
00:16:31,620 --> 00:16:34,060
which in turn are surrounded by a stationary gear.

219
00:16:34,940 --> 00:16:40,460
As the sun gear rotates, it forces the planet gears to walk around the stationary gear.

220
00:16:41,260 --> 00:16:44,540
With this gearing down, it takes less effort to turn the wheels.

221
00:16:49,020 --> 00:16:53,380
Monster truck driver Frank Scatini of Los Angeles used to be a motocross racer.

222
00:16:54,520 --> 00:17:00,180
His motorcycle experience led him to design and build one of the most unusual monster trucks ever made.

223
00:17:00,181 --> 00:17:10,580
Frank's truck, Monster Jerky, has a stand-up driver's position, a first in the industry.

224
00:17:11,480 --> 00:17:14,340
The standing-up position has worked out very well for me.

225
00:17:14,800 --> 00:17:16,760
My advantage is more visibility.

226
00:17:18,140 --> 00:17:21,580
I have more control of the truck. Everything's more second nature to me.

227
00:17:21,840 --> 00:17:26,060
If you look down to my left foot, I have a shifter, just like a motorcycle.

228
00:17:26,860 --> 00:17:29,440
I upshift and I downshift.

229
00:17:29,441 --> 00:17:32,360
On my right hand, this is my finger throttle.

230
00:17:35,820 --> 00:17:39,120
On my left hand, I have my front brakes.

231
00:17:41,120 --> 00:17:44,600
With my left thumb, I can steer my rear steering.

232
00:17:45,040 --> 00:17:46,460
This would be for left steer.

233
00:17:47,020 --> 00:17:48,280
This would be for right steer.

234
00:17:48,440 --> 00:17:49,960
I don't have to remove my hand.

235
00:17:50,040 --> 00:17:50,960
It's in full function.

236
00:17:51,480 --> 00:17:53,740
I can brake and steer at the same time.

237
00:17:53,840 --> 00:17:59,400
The stand-up position allows Frank's legs to act as additional shock absorbers in hard landings.

238
00:17:59,440 --> 00:18:04,020
So far, Frank's unique system hasn't won over any converts among other drivers.

239
00:18:04,440 --> 00:18:07,800
When Rick Swanson tried driving Frank's truck, he found it a challenge.

240
00:18:07,801 --> 00:18:14,000
I would say I'm probably six inches taller than Frank and over 100 pounds heavier than Frank,

241
00:18:14,140 --> 00:18:18,320
so I don't exactly fit in the truck as well as he does.

242
00:18:19,180 --> 00:18:24,640
I ended up doing a faceplant on the dash and almost hitting the car behind me.

243
00:18:25,340 --> 00:18:28,300
Another one-of-a-kind truck is Bigfoot 5.

244
00:18:29,440 --> 00:18:34,720
It has tires that are nearly twice as big as the agricultural tires that racing monster trucks use.

245
00:18:35,760 --> 00:18:37,540
Bigfoot 5 is for exhibition only.

246
00:18:38,140 --> 00:18:43,000
It can't compete in the ruck-and-tumble races and jumps, but it can crush cars.

247
00:18:43,720 --> 00:18:46,520
And it is the tallest pickup truck in the world.

248
00:18:48,740 --> 00:18:53,980
Bob Chandler found Bigfoot 5's huge tires and wheels in a junkyard in Seattle.

249
00:18:54,980 --> 00:18:58,020
They came from one of the most amazing trucks ever built,

250
00:18:58,500 --> 00:19:01,400
a U.S. military transport called the Snow Train.

251
00:19:01,960 --> 00:19:04,953
It was built for the Army in the mid-50s by the R.G.

252
00:19:04,954 --> 00:19:07,140
Letourneau Company of Longview, Texas.

253
00:19:09,480 --> 00:19:13,160
It was designed to carry supplies to remote Arctic locations,

254
00:19:13,760 --> 00:19:16,100
like the distant early warning or dew line,

255
00:19:16,520 --> 00:19:19,460
a string of military radar locations across the far north.

256
00:19:19,461 --> 00:19:25,460
The snow train had individual electric motors to drive each of its 16 wheels.

257
00:19:25,461 --> 00:19:30,460
Electric power was provided by two onboard gas turbine generators.

258
00:19:30,461 --> 00:19:37,460
Its gigantic wheels and tires were 10 feet high and 4 feet wide.

259
00:19:37,461 --> 00:19:47,460
They could carry the snow train and cargo of up to 100,000 pounds over deep snow that a man on foot or a smaller vehicle would sink into.

260
00:19:47,461 --> 00:19:52,540
The snow train had sleeping quarters, a kitchen, and a bathroom for its crew.

261
00:19:53,400 --> 00:19:59,000
It was used by the Army for only seven years, making its last supply trip in 1962.

262
00:20:00,220 --> 00:20:04,960
Its rusting wheels and huge tires were the largest Bob Chandler had ever seen,

263
00:20:05,620 --> 00:20:07,660
and he knew he had to have them.

264
00:20:08,240 --> 00:20:11,660
And I saw these, and one way or another, I'm going to make them work on the truck.

265
00:20:11,800 --> 00:20:14,460
The initial thing we did actually stood outside the body of the truck,

266
00:20:14,461 --> 00:20:17,500
and they worked that way for quite a while

267
00:20:17,501 --> 00:20:19,720
and it was so hard on the bearings of the truck

268
00:20:19,721 --> 00:20:21,040
by extending them out that far

269
00:20:21,041 --> 00:20:23,240
that we end up building a truck just far of those tires

270
00:20:23,241 --> 00:20:25,740
and raising it up so the tires were sucked in closer

271
00:20:25,741 --> 00:20:27,760
and then they handled it much better.

272
00:20:28,400 --> 00:20:30,820
But driving Bigfoot 5 is no easy matter.

273
00:20:32,240 --> 00:20:34,920
You know, it's so awkward and it's so hard to see.

274
00:20:35,120 --> 00:20:37,300
When we do a parade, you've got to have four people on the ground

275
00:20:37,301 --> 00:20:38,840
walking alongside of it

276
00:20:38,841 --> 00:20:40,720
because the driver can't see people up close to it.

277
00:20:40,721 --> 00:20:46,720
It has been turned to the greatest truck-truck gathering on the planet.

278
00:20:47,260 --> 00:20:51,120
And now it returns to the Indiana State Fairgrounds.

279
00:20:51,121 --> 00:20:56,600
Don't miss the 19th Annual Advanced Auto Parts Fall 4-Wheel Jammery Nationals.

280
00:20:57,420 --> 00:21:03,200
One of the biggest annual monster truck races in the country takes place in Indianapolis every September.

281
00:21:04,100 --> 00:21:08,020
It's three days of total immersion in the world of 4-Wheel Drive trucks.

282
00:21:08,480 --> 00:21:10,220
And the monsters are the star attraction.

283
00:21:10,720 --> 00:21:14,100
Some of the top monster trucks and drivers are racing here.

284
00:21:14,760 --> 00:21:17,100
The Bigfoot organization is racing two trucks,

285
00:21:17,580 --> 00:21:20,480
Bigfoot 14, driven by record setter Dan Runty,

286
00:21:21,180 --> 00:21:23,500
and Bigfoot Tonka, driven by Eric Tack.

287
00:21:24,700 --> 00:21:28,520
Steve Macklin, the owner-driver of Nitro Fish, is relatively new to the game.

288
00:21:29,060 --> 00:21:31,080
He's been racing for only about a year and a half.

289
00:21:31,700 --> 00:21:34,020
I've raced with all the trucks here this weekend

290
00:21:34,021 --> 00:21:36,780
and been able to come out on top in a few cases

291
00:21:36,781 --> 00:21:39,340
and been able to not come out on top in a few cases.

292
00:21:39,341 --> 00:21:42,020
So the truck is more than capable of doing it.

293
00:21:42,080 --> 00:21:43,960
I think I have as good a chance as anybody.

294
00:21:44,460 --> 00:21:48,640
Steve, a nitro fish has been actually getting better every race.

295
00:21:48,820 --> 00:21:52,060
He's had some great races so far, and he's had a few that he's had problems with.

296
00:21:52,520 --> 00:21:54,140
So I think he's going to come around.

297
00:21:54,200 --> 00:21:55,900
He's going to be one of the great competitors out here.

298
00:21:56,480 --> 00:22:02,100
A guy that took his driver's test here this weekend, ladies and gentlemen,

299
00:22:02,520 --> 00:22:07,520
he's going to come run with the big boys, giving up for the big dog and dog, Milky!

300
00:22:09,340 --> 00:22:12,520
Doug Nelke is the ultimate rookie at this race.

301
00:22:12,960 --> 00:22:18,300
Just a few hours ago, he took his monster truck driver's test in front of several experienced drivers

302
00:22:18,301 --> 00:22:21,860
who had to decide if he was ready for his Class A license.

303
00:22:23,200 --> 00:22:28,180
Doug passed the test, proving he knows how to handle Big Dog, the new truck he spent months building.

304
00:22:28,780 --> 00:22:31,000
I was one of the inspectors that signed him off.

305
00:22:31,080 --> 00:22:33,960
If he's smart, he'll drive his own race this weekend.

306
00:22:33,961 --> 00:22:38,260
He won't try to just blow everybody away and show everybody he's good, he got good, because it takes time.

307
00:22:38,261 --> 00:22:43,660
For Doug Nelke, becoming a monster truck driver is the realization of a childhood dream.

308
00:22:44,460 --> 00:22:46,540
Things were awesome. Impressed me when I was a little kid.

309
00:22:47,140 --> 00:22:49,420
Just something I always wanted to do.

310
00:22:49,600 --> 00:22:52,140
Used to mud race and finally got our monster truck.

311
00:22:52,200 --> 00:22:54,240
Dreams coming true, I tell you. It's a great time.

312
00:22:54,520 --> 00:22:57,440
Many of the other drivers, like Eric Tack of the Bigfoot team,

313
00:22:57,840 --> 00:23:00,920
take time to give rookies like Doug the benefit of their experience.

314
00:23:01,700 --> 00:23:05,760
Tack recalls a time when the steering wheel came off in his hand in the middle of a race.

315
00:23:05,761 --> 00:23:08,460
You do it a hundred times, you just take it for granted.

316
00:23:08,640 --> 00:23:09,340
Everything's there.

317
00:23:09,920 --> 00:23:11,540
You out hit the cars, you're up in the air going.

318
00:23:13,580 --> 00:23:15,160
That would be a bad thing.

319
00:23:18,860 --> 00:23:22,120
Most monster truck races are run on a straight line course.

320
00:23:22,780 --> 00:23:23,860
But this one is different.

321
00:23:24,680 --> 00:23:28,340
Called an S-track, it has multiple turns and is much more challenging.

322
00:23:29,720 --> 00:23:33,120
The two drivers with the least experience seem the most concerned.

323
00:23:33,400 --> 00:23:35,620
Something I've never done, so it's all new to me.

324
00:23:35,760 --> 00:23:38,660
So we've done straight line racing, you know, over sets of cars.

325
00:23:38,840 --> 00:23:42,880
The S course is a little bit more of a challenge for me just because of, I think, of lack of experience.

326
00:23:43,220 --> 00:23:45,200
So that'll present a little bit of a challenge.

327
00:23:45,360 --> 00:23:48,640
A lot of the S track stuff is driving skill.

328
00:23:48,641 --> 00:23:52,480
I mean, it's more driver skill than just going out and running in a straight line.

329
00:23:52,740 --> 00:23:57,580
There's a lot that can go wrong and a lot that you have to be ready to correct for.

330
00:23:57,820 --> 00:24:03,440
So just once you feel comfortable, once you're through the turn, look up and find your cars and then make a flight adjustment over there.

331
00:24:03,441 --> 00:24:06,740
I mean, I can say that, I may not do it.

332
00:24:09,480 --> 00:24:13,360
I may be sideways coming through here going, oh, I should have listened to myself.

333
00:24:14,200 --> 00:24:21,480
They may be fierce competitors on the track, but monster truck drivers are known for helping each other out and treating one another like family.

334
00:24:21,481 --> 00:24:28,080
The thing that I found is the people that I go to the races with, if you're in dire need of trouble, they're over giving you a hand.

335
00:24:28,081 --> 00:24:33,980
So the guy you may be racing in the next round is probably helping you out or maybe even loaning your parts and vice versa.

336
00:24:34,180 --> 00:24:34,916
These guys are great.

337
00:24:34,917 --> 00:24:39,440
I mean, you meet all these new people and they're all just, I mean, it's like your best buddies, you know, and you just met them.

338
00:24:39,860 --> 00:24:41,480
It's awesome. It really is.

339
00:24:41,820 --> 00:24:48,900
As they get ready for the first race, Dan Runty tells Bob Chandler he's concerned about the slippery grass areas on the S-curves.

340
00:24:49,060 --> 00:24:50,820
I don't like these tires on this grass.

341
00:24:52,320 --> 00:24:53,280
Very hard, Tony.

342
00:24:53,281 --> 00:24:53,981
Yeah.

343
00:24:53,982 --> 00:25:01,240
But at this race, slipping tires will turn out to be the least of the driver's worries.

344
00:25:02,520 --> 00:25:05,320
Coming up, the big race is full of big problems.

345
00:25:07,120 --> 00:25:11,360
An estimated 4 to 5 million people attend monster truck races every year,

346
00:25:11,361 --> 00:25:15,840
more than U.S. Major League Soccer or the Women's National Basketball Association.

347
00:25:17,340 --> 00:25:19,800
Monster Trucks will return on Modern Marvels.

348
00:25:23,640 --> 00:25:28,000
One of the most crucial ingredients of a successful monster truck race is dirt.

349
00:25:29,700 --> 00:25:33,420
They're specialists in making the right kind of dirt and mud.

350
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,760
There's a specialist who comes in a couple days early

351
00:25:36,761 --> 00:25:41,000
and makes sure that dirt is just the right consistency for monster trucks

352
00:25:41,001 --> 00:25:45,460
or for mud racers, which sometimes go along with the monster truck racing.

353
00:25:45,461 --> 00:25:49,460
So it's becoming an interesting world with all sorts of fascinating occupations

354
00:25:50,120 --> 00:25:51,600
like dirt specialist.

355
00:25:51,601 --> 00:25:58,320
stadiums become dirt tracks overnight with as many as a hundred truckloads of dirt brought in

356
00:25:58,321 --> 00:26:04,560
at a cost of up to a hundred thousand dollars it can't be too sandy or contain too many rocks

357
00:26:04,561 --> 00:26:11,800
and the best dirt has a high clay content some race promoters own dirt in several cities

358
00:26:11,801 --> 00:26:19,340
which they truck into various venues the dirt is sprayed with water before a race to keep down the

359
00:26:19,341 --> 00:26:26,864
dust. Safety is of paramount importance at every monster truck event.

360
00:26:26,865 --> 00:26:28,500
If a truck this

361
00:26:28,501 --> 00:26:33,960
big, powerful, and virtually unstoppable ever got out of control, the results could be disastrous.

362
00:26:35,320 --> 00:26:41,480
Please, everyone, stay back. Please, stay back. Everyone, please, stay back. Let the

363
00:26:41,481 --> 00:26:46,950
officials get next to the truck. They would and they have driven over each other.

364
00:26:46,951 --> 00:26:47,651
I've

365
00:26:47,652 --> 00:26:50,420
got a video of one of my trucks driving over another monster truck.

366
00:26:50,421 --> 00:26:51,280
He went clean over

367
00:26:51,281 --> 00:26:54,647
the top of that monster truck and coasted to a stop on the other side.

368
00:26:54,648 --> 00:26:55,348
And it's just,

369
00:26:55,460 --> 00:26:57,274
it's unbelievable that it could do that.

370
00:26:57,275 --> 00:26:59,360
Dug over a four foot wall like it's not there.

371
00:27:00,460 --> 00:27:05,040
What scared me from the beginning was that they would drive up into the seats of a hockey

372
00:27:05,041 --> 00:27:05,741
arena.

373
00:27:05,742 --> 00:27:08,840
And if the driver has knocked himself out and put his foot to the floor, they would

374
00:27:08,841 --> 00:27:09,934
go all the way up those seats.

375
00:27:09,935 --> 00:27:11,940
They would go to the wall of the building and then roll

376
00:27:11,941 --> 00:27:12,641
back down.

377
00:27:13,060 --> 00:27:16,640
Tragically, there have been two deaths of bystanders at monster truck races,

378
00:27:16,820 --> 00:27:22,000
but both occurred before a new system of engine cut-off switches was fully developed.

379
00:27:23,360 --> 00:27:27,480
To guard against an out-of-control vehicle becoming an unstoppable juggernaut,

380
00:27:27,920 --> 00:27:30,400
there are now three cut-off switches for every truck.

381
00:27:31,760 --> 00:27:36,960
During a race, two track officials each hold in their hands a remote radio-controlled switch.

382
00:27:37,640 --> 00:27:41,080
They're called RIIs, remote ignition interrupters.

383
00:27:42,460 --> 00:27:47,200
These two switches can instantly kill the engines of the trucks racing.

384
00:27:48,300 --> 00:27:54,180
It's nothing more than a walkie-talkie connected to a unit in a truck that just totally kills all the power.

385
00:27:54,660 --> 00:27:56,600
It turns off all the electrical power of that truck.

386
00:27:57,100 --> 00:27:59,720
It turns off the fuel pumps, it turns off everything that makes it run.

387
00:28:00,480 --> 00:28:03,900
There is also a clearly marked cutoff switch at the back of every truck,

388
00:28:04,040 --> 00:28:07,640
which can be pulled by the first track employee at the scene of an accident.

389
00:28:09,300 --> 00:28:11,960
And the drivers have a cutoff switch inside the cockpit.

390
00:28:12,460 --> 00:28:17,720
When they roll or crash a truck, the drivers hit the cutoff switch immediately.

391
00:28:18,400 --> 00:28:23,580
But in many cases, the engine is already dead, turned off by the track official holding the RII.

392
00:28:24,900 --> 00:28:27,060
It's happening today through Sunday.

393
00:28:27,520 --> 00:28:33,880
Don't miss the return of Foster Trucks' side-by-side racing action of a new Chalatin S-Course with 12 trucks.

394
00:28:33,881 --> 00:28:41,980
Although the monster trucks are the main attraction, the four-wheel jamboree here in Indianapolis is a celebration of the truck in all shapes and sizes.

395
00:28:42,460 --> 00:28:46,680
One of the most unusual trucks on display here, and definitely the biggest,

396
00:28:47,120 --> 00:28:51,360
is this twin-engine Kenworth semi-rig called the CFI Red Racer.

397
00:28:52,580 --> 00:28:58,460
Its engines put out a total of 3,000 horsepower and 7,000 foot-pounds of torque.

398
00:28:59,460 --> 00:29:02,620
We modified this truck into making it a drag race truck,

399
00:29:03,420 --> 00:29:08,340
and running air shows and drag races, and we take it to all the major truck shows all over the country.

400
00:29:09,160 --> 00:29:12,700
This truck right now is running low 13s at 110 and a quarter mile.

401
00:29:22,140 --> 00:29:26,200
With the right set of gears now, I think this truck's probably capable of running close to 200 miles an hour.

402
00:29:26,920 --> 00:29:29,340
That's a lot faster than the monster trucks can go.

403
00:29:30,200 --> 00:29:34,460
In their races this weekend, the top speed will be about 60 miles an hour.

404
00:29:34,461 --> 00:29:38,740
Before a race, monster trucks often do a car crush.

405
00:29:39,220 --> 00:29:41,180
But today, they've come up with a new twist.

406
00:29:41,820 --> 00:29:45,860
This Korean import is about to feel the power of the two Bigfoot trucks.

407
00:29:55,040 --> 00:30:01,620
One of the best drivers on the circuit told me that he said the appeal of this sport is the destructivity.

408
00:30:02,060 --> 00:30:06,480
When the racing finally gets underway, the drivers pull up to the starting line

409
00:30:06,481 --> 00:30:12,300
and raise their arms to signal they're ready for the remote ignition interrupter switch, or RII, to be tested.

410
00:30:13,680 --> 00:30:18,060
After the engines are killed and then restarted, the two trucks are ready to race.

411
00:30:19,800 --> 00:30:24,600
Winner goes on to the next bracket, and he races the next truck that wins the second bracket.

412
00:30:25,240 --> 00:30:30,320
And whoever sets the fastest time in their brackets gets lane choice for the next race,

413
00:30:30,321 --> 00:30:31,900
which is very important.

414
00:30:32,020 --> 00:30:35,100
Some tracks have advantages and disadvantages for different tracks.

415
00:30:35,980 --> 00:30:39,780
Although some events have purses, most, including this one, do not.

416
00:30:40,480 --> 00:30:43,460
The monster truck owners are usually paid a flat show-up fee,

417
00:30:43,900 --> 00:30:45,500
no matter how well they do in the race.

418
00:30:46,040 --> 00:30:48,600
This has led to criticism that it's not real racing,

419
00:30:49,060 --> 00:30:51,340
but the drivers maintain they compete fiercely,

420
00:30:51,740 --> 00:30:53,060
no matter how they're being paid.

421
00:30:53,720 --> 00:30:56,040
We're not racing for a direct purse,

422
00:30:56,280 --> 00:30:58,020
but we're racing for an indirect purse.

423
00:30:58,021 --> 00:31:01,940
That show-up fee is based on last year's schedule.

424
00:31:02,380 --> 00:31:06,760
How many events you did, how well you did, who saw you.

425
00:31:07,260 --> 00:31:11,100
But the reason you want to do good once you're there, obviously for next year,

426
00:31:11,640 --> 00:31:14,320
and it'll also help T-shirt sales that night.

427
00:31:14,540 --> 00:31:19,120
T-shirts can range anywhere from $200 to $1,000 in a weekend.

428
00:31:20,100 --> 00:31:21,000
Extra money.

429
00:31:22,280 --> 00:31:26,820
The high-performance monster truck engines need a lot of downtime between races.

430
00:31:27,340 --> 00:31:29,720
It's not like circle track racing where guys can go 100 laps.

431
00:31:29,820 --> 00:31:32,860
The alcohol, you know, supercharged engines need some time to breathe.

432
00:31:32,920 --> 00:31:35,140
It's more like drag racing as far as the engines are set up.

433
00:31:35,460 --> 00:31:37,240
You've got to have a chance for them to cool down,

434
00:31:37,720 --> 00:31:40,520
and you've got to have a chance for them to kind of regroup a little bit.

435
00:31:41,480 --> 00:31:44,660
The monsters are usually interspersed with other kinds of racing,

436
00:31:45,220 --> 00:31:47,460
like these mud dragsters.

437
00:31:51,060 --> 00:31:56,580
Ladies and gentlemen, we are about five minutes away from class number five.

438
00:31:56,820 --> 00:32:00,480
And the guys that were competing here this afternoon in our Monster Truck Thunder Drags,

439
00:32:00,481 --> 00:32:06,360
the Monster Truck Autograph Session will be held immediately following Monster Truck Thunder Drags competition.

440
00:32:06,780 --> 00:32:07,480
There you go, buddy.

441
00:32:09,400 --> 00:32:15,580
One of the most important parts of a Monster Truck driver's job is interacting with the enthusiastic fans.

442
00:32:16,240 --> 00:32:22,860
The drivers make themselves available for autographs, pictures, before and after the event.

443
00:32:23,980 --> 00:32:26,660
We were there until the last fan leaves.

444
00:32:26,820 --> 00:32:31,220
with either signing t-shirts, signing pictures, whatever.

445
00:32:31,600 --> 00:32:35,140
The drivers of monster trucks have become sort of like NASCAR drivers.

446
00:32:35,300 --> 00:32:38,120
They used to be these gritty old daredevil types,

447
00:32:38,240 --> 00:32:39,880
and now they're very articulate people

448
00:32:39,881 --> 00:32:43,600
who are always very careful to mention the sponsors in their interviews.

449
00:32:43,820 --> 00:32:46,560
They're very polished performers and very media savvy.

450
00:32:49,060 --> 00:32:49,900
A prickle?

451
00:32:50,460 --> 00:32:52,300
A prickle? There you go, buddy.

452
00:32:53,000 --> 00:32:53,960
Okay, get back.

453
00:32:53,961 --> 00:32:56,460
The kids love getting the driver's autographs.

454
00:32:56,820 --> 00:32:59,720
But sometimes, kids say the darndest things.

455
00:33:00,260 --> 00:33:03,480
We've done an autograph session, and the kid getting up next to the line,

456
00:33:03,560 --> 00:33:05,500
and his dad said, that's the driver, so-and-so.

457
00:33:05,780 --> 00:33:08,900
And the kid goes, well, he sucks, he breaks all the time.

458
00:33:10,000 --> 00:33:14,280
Which was 100% true, but it was like, oh, out of the mouth of babes, you know.

459
00:33:14,340 --> 00:33:16,340
You don't expect kids to be that blunt.

460
00:33:17,660 --> 00:33:22,820
For the most part, the people are after the autograph of almost the truck.

461
00:33:22,980 --> 00:33:25,960
It's not so much the driver, it's almost the truck.

462
00:33:26,820 --> 00:33:32,240
After the first day's races, there are complaints that the Indianapolis track isn't challenging enough.

463
00:33:32,880 --> 00:33:36,960
Most of the drivers had the same impression that the track was just lame.

464
00:33:37,140 --> 00:33:39,240
It just wasn't exciting enough for the crowd.

465
00:33:40,500 --> 00:33:45,860
We had a talk with the promoter, and the promoter agreed that there was something that had to be done.

466
00:33:46,880 --> 00:33:50,940
The promoter decides to make some of the jumps steeper for the next day's racing.

467
00:33:51,920 --> 00:33:58,620
They will also plow up some of the grassy areas to give the trucks more traction, allowing them to turn the corners faster.

468
00:33:59,620 --> 00:34:02,360
There will be more challenges and more excitement tomorrow.

469
00:34:03,180 --> 00:34:06,220
In fact, much more than anyone is expecting.

470
00:34:09,220 --> 00:34:15,060
In the last 10 years, sales of Bigfoot licensed products have totaled more than $250 million.

471
00:34:16,720 --> 00:34:19,540
Monster Trucks will return on Modern Marvels.

472
00:34:20,940 --> 00:34:28,640
The final day of monster truck racing is about to begin at the Four Wheel Jamboree in Indianapolis, Indiana.

473
00:34:29,360 --> 00:34:35,560
As recorded by our four cameras, it will be a day that turns into a simultaneous, multi-level drama.

474
00:34:36,240 --> 00:34:40,560
A day that truly captures the thrills, spills, and chills of monster truck racing.

475
00:34:42,680 --> 00:34:47,620
The track has been changed since the previous day to make the jumps higher and more exciting.

476
00:34:47,621 --> 00:34:51,040
they will prove to be much more difficult for the drivers

477
00:34:51,041 --> 00:35:02,060
in the first race the rookie Doug Nelke in Big Dog

478
00:35:02,061 --> 00:35:05,280
faces off against one of the most experienced drivers

479
00:35:05,281 --> 00:35:07,260
Dan Runty in Bigfoot

480
00:35:07,261 --> 00:35:10,540
Runty's experience wins out

481
00:35:12,540 --> 00:35:15,520
in the next round Dan Runty is again victorious

482
00:35:16,120 --> 00:35:17,800
this time against Monster Patrol.

483
00:35:18,500 --> 00:35:20,380
But the victory comes at a price.

484
00:35:21,220 --> 00:35:25,800
At the very end of his run, Runty's Bigfoot breaks down and can't make it off the field.

485
00:35:26,660 --> 00:35:31,400
At first, Bigfoot owner Bob Chandler and his crew believe it's a transmission problem.

486
00:35:32,180 --> 00:35:34,100
We've got a lot of work to do to get this out of there.

487
00:35:34,500 --> 00:35:37,260
They can change it in a half hour when the conditions are right,

488
00:35:37,320 --> 00:35:40,480
but being hot like this and not having all their tools, this might take a little longer.

489
00:35:40,481 --> 00:35:41,426
happening there.

490
00:35:41,427 --> 00:35:45,740
As the Bigfoot crew work on their disabled truck at the end of the field,

491
00:35:46,340 --> 00:35:51,860
their teammate Eric Tack in the Bigfoot Tonka truck and Steve Macklin in Nitrofish get ready

492
00:35:51,861 --> 00:35:52,561
to race.

493
00:35:52,562 --> 00:35:58,540
Our onboard camera on Nitrofish is about to capture the kind of race every monster truck

494
00:35:58,541 --> 00:36:04,557
driver hopes to avoid. As they blast off, Steve Macklin seems to be doing well.

495
00:36:04,558 --> 00:36:05,700
But then on the

496
00:36:05,701 --> 00:36:12,700
first jump something goes wrong nitro fish lands hard on its nose

497
00:36:15,020 --> 00:36:19,580
Dan Runty and Bob Chandler wince when they see it

498
00:36:19,581 --> 00:36:24,740
they know it must have been a painful landing

499
00:36:24,741 --> 00:36:27,760
the camera shows the jolt on Steve's body

500
00:36:27,761 --> 00:36:32,480
landings like this can cause serious neck and back injuries

501
00:36:32,481 --> 00:36:36,360
But Steve at first appears to be fine, and he continues driving the course.

502
00:36:37,540 --> 00:36:42,760
But seconds later, as he drives off the field at the end of the race, he is clearly having problems.

503
00:36:43,900 --> 00:36:48,100
Although the fans and other drivers don't know it yet, Steve Macklin is injured.

504
00:36:49,400 --> 00:36:56,580
At the end of the field, Nitro Fish comes to a sudden stop, but Steve doesn't get out, and he doesn't respond to radio calls.

505
00:36:57,380 --> 00:36:59,540
Paramedics are at his side just seconds later.

506
00:36:59,541 --> 00:37:01,660
Can you get out? You with me?

507
00:37:02,480 --> 00:37:03,480
You with me?

508
00:37:03,481 --> 00:37:05,480
How you feeling up there?

509
00:37:05,481 --> 00:37:06,480
You alright?

510
00:37:06,481 --> 00:37:07,480
You have any problems breathing?

511
00:37:07,481 --> 00:37:08,480
No.

512
00:37:08,481 --> 00:37:10,480
Can you tell me where you're hurting?

513
00:37:10,481 --> 00:37:11,480
Here.

514
00:37:11,481 --> 00:37:12,480
How you feeling?

515
00:37:12,481 --> 00:37:13,480
He's alert.

516
00:37:13,481 --> 00:37:14,480
He's alert.

517
00:37:14,481 --> 00:37:15,480
He's alert.

518
00:37:15,481 --> 00:37:16,480
Where you hurting at?

519
00:37:16,481 --> 00:37:17,480
My neck.

520
00:37:17,481 --> 00:37:18,480
Your neck?

521
00:37:18,481 --> 00:37:19,480
Yeah.

522
00:37:19,481 --> 00:37:20,480
Neck and back a little bit.

523
00:37:20,481 --> 00:37:21,480
Your neck and the...

524
00:37:21,481 --> 00:37:22,480
Leave your helmet on, okay, for me?

525
00:37:22,481 --> 00:37:23,480
Okay.

526
00:37:23,481 --> 00:37:24,480
Find out where we got.

527
00:37:24,481 --> 00:37:25,480
Step right in there.

528
00:37:25,481 --> 00:37:27,480
He's conscious, alert, and oriented.

529
00:37:27,481 --> 00:37:29,480
He's got neck and back pain, dude.

530
00:37:29,481 --> 00:37:30,480
Neck and back pain.

531
00:37:30,481 --> 00:37:31,480
Neck and back pain.

532
00:37:31,481 --> 00:37:32,860
Grab my fingers and squeeze.

533
00:37:33,980 --> 00:37:39,240
As they work on their own truck, the Bigfoot crew gets the word that Steve Macklin has been injured.

534
00:37:39,900 --> 00:37:40,700
Still in the truck.

535
00:37:42,380 --> 00:37:43,940
He stayed in the truck down there.

536
00:37:44,120 --> 00:37:44,820
He's still in there.

537
00:37:45,460 --> 00:37:49,100
As far as I can see, when he went over to first jump there, the truck seemed to nose out real hard.

538
00:37:49,200 --> 00:37:51,300
I mean, he probably whipped his neck pretty good forward.

539
00:37:51,301 --> 00:37:53,240
That's what it looked, and the truck bounced pretty violently.

540
00:37:54,200 --> 00:37:58,700
Doug Nelke says he found the jumps much steeper today, perhaps too steep.

541
00:37:58,701 --> 00:38:01,880
They changed him here right before the race.

542
00:38:02,000 --> 00:38:03,320
I mean, it made him a lot taller,

543
00:38:03,540 --> 00:38:06,280
and I don't know if that was really the right thing to be doing, you know.

544
00:38:06,460 --> 00:38:08,240
It's sky in the trucks, pretty much.

545
00:38:08,380 --> 00:38:10,660
I mean, it's pretty wild.

546
00:38:11,040 --> 00:38:12,020
Slow as you want to go.

547
00:38:13,220 --> 00:38:14,620
Here, use me if you need to, buddy.

548
00:38:14,840 --> 00:38:15,540
Okay?

549
00:38:16,480 --> 00:38:19,680
The paramedics help Steve Macklin to climb out of his truck.

550
00:38:23,600 --> 00:38:26,040
Later, as he receives oxygen in the ambulance,

551
00:38:26,540 --> 00:38:28,660
he insists he doesn't want to go to the hospital.

552
00:38:28,700 --> 00:38:41,140
out on the track the racing continues in the final round executioner driven by mark hall

553
00:38:41,141 --> 00:38:42,700
emerges the victor

554
00:38:42,701 --> 00:38:54,680
at the end of the field work on bigfoot continues while steve macklin is still resisting the idea

555
00:38:54,681 --> 00:38:55,760
of going to the hospital.

556
00:38:55,920 --> 00:38:57,100
Let's go get it checked out, man.

557
00:38:57,260 --> 00:38:57,960
It ain't gonna hurt.

558
00:38:58,960 --> 00:38:59,980
Just gotta help me lay down.

559
00:39:00,000 --> 00:39:00,700
Yeah.

560
00:39:00,701 --> 00:39:01,401
Come on.

561
00:39:01,402 --> 00:39:02,102
I'll bet you.

562
00:39:03,640 --> 00:39:04,500
That's all I need.

563
00:39:04,760 --> 00:39:05,460
Thank you.

564
00:39:05,620 --> 00:39:06,760
Need some more ice or anything?

565
00:39:06,900 --> 00:39:08,240
No, I gotta come over there.

566
00:39:08,380 --> 00:39:09,080
It's nothing, right?

567
00:39:13,220 --> 00:39:14,740
Although the racing is over,

568
00:39:14,741 --> 00:39:17,100
the most exciting action is yet to come.

569
00:39:18,140 --> 00:39:20,100
The climax of every monster truck event

570
00:39:20,101 --> 00:39:21,080
is the freestyle,

571
00:39:21,660 --> 00:39:23,520
where drivers go out on the field solo

572
00:39:23,521 --> 00:39:25,500
and push their trucks to the limits.

573
00:39:32,360 --> 00:39:35,520
Doug Nelke in Big Dog pulls out all the stops.

574
00:39:36,180 --> 00:39:39,600
For a rookie who got his driver's license just 36 hours ago,

575
00:39:39,940 --> 00:39:41,160
it's a knockout performance.

576
00:39:41,740 --> 00:39:45,020
But in the process, Doug comes close to knocking himself out.

577
00:39:45,740 --> 00:39:47,740
I went over to first jump. That was okay.

578
00:39:47,840 --> 00:39:49,820
And then when I did front of them cars, I skied up,

579
00:39:49,821 --> 00:39:51,640
and then I jumped down in that hole, and I guess it bounced.

580
00:39:51,641 --> 00:39:52,341
smog.

581
00:39:52,342 --> 00:39:55,800
The front of the helmet hit the steering wheel, so that's how far that head went.

582
00:39:58,880 --> 00:40:03,660
And now it's time for Eric Tack, one of the most experienced drivers on the Bigfoot team,

583
00:40:03,661 --> 00:40:04,880
to do his freestyle.

584
00:40:04,881 --> 00:40:18,881
Eric's performance is a tour de force.

585
00:40:23,860 --> 00:40:26,960
He is a master of the wheelie, and he proves it tonight.

586
00:40:30,760 --> 00:40:32,960
It is a freestyle to be remembered.

587
00:40:34,880 --> 00:40:44,880
After watching Eric wow the crowd, Bob Chandler walks back to the pit area to check on the injured Steve Macklin.

588
00:40:52,640 --> 00:40:58,160
Steve's left arm is still numb, and he finally agrees to let ambulance attendants take him to the hospital.

589
00:41:04,880 --> 00:41:14,060
At 9 p.m., as Steve Macklin is getting tests at the hospital,

590
00:41:14,700 --> 00:41:19,060
Bob Chandler, Dan Ronte, and Eric Tack are still out in the dark, deserted field,

591
00:41:19,540 --> 00:41:21,260
trying to fix Bigfoot 14.

592
00:41:22,180 --> 00:41:22,940
Here's what happened.

593
00:41:24,060 --> 00:41:25,220
Broke the U-joint in the axle.

594
00:41:26,220 --> 00:41:27,300
That's the U-joint right there.

595
00:41:27,540 --> 00:41:28,300
That was the weak link.

596
00:41:28,440 --> 00:41:29,760
It was found the weak link and broke it.

597
00:41:29,761 --> 00:41:35,960
Meanwhile at the hospital, doctors have discovered that Steve Macklin has a ruptured disc.

598
00:41:36,480 --> 00:41:40,860
They tell him surgery may be necessary eventually, but it's not urgently needed.

599
00:41:43,080 --> 00:41:48,940
One week later, in Daytona, Florida, Steve Macklin and Nitrofish are back in action.

600
00:41:49,600 --> 00:41:53,480
But this time, Steve is wearing something called the hands restraining helmet,

601
00:41:53,960 --> 00:41:55,880
lent to him by Bigfoot's Bob Chandler.

602
00:41:56,080 --> 00:41:59,700
The letters H-A-N-S stand for head and neck support.

603
00:41:59,760 --> 00:42:05,320
The helmet is used by the Bigfoot team for special stunts like the 202 foot

604
00:42:05,321 --> 00:42:11,000
jump Dan Runty made over a 727 airliner. The helmet is attached to a collar and

605
00:42:11,001 --> 00:42:15,440
yoke system worn on the upper body under the driver's shoulder straps. It's

606
00:42:15,441 --> 00:42:19,220
designed to prevent stress on the driver's neck and spine by limiting the

607
00:42:19,221 --> 00:42:23,880
movement of the head in relation to the body. Steve Macklin is going to need it

608
00:42:23,881 --> 00:42:27,140
for what's about to happen.

609
00:42:29,760 --> 00:42:34,180
Near the end of his first run, Steve's truck suddenly flips over on its roof.

610
00:42:34,720 --> 00:42:37,940
But then it keeps on going and lands back on its wheels.

611
00:42:40,160 --> 00:42:42,220
Steve comes through it without a scratch.

612
00:42:43,260 --> 00:42:47,720
The truck has lost some of its body panels, but it can still race without them.

613
00:42:49,480 --> 00:42:54,760
It's all part of the game in the rough and tumble, unpredictable world of monster truck racing.

614
00:42:55,520 --> 00:42:57,860
It's a world with an equally unpredictable future.

615
00:42:58,760 --> 00:43:01,620
The industry appears headed in two different directions.

616
00:43:02,320 --> 00:43:03,860
One is toward more showmanship.

617
00:43:04,500 --> 00:43:06,420
The other is toward more legitimate racing,

618
00:43:06,720 --> 00:43:10,000
with bigger purses to winners instead of flat fees to drivers.

619
00:43:11,120 --> 00:43:13,100
We might end up being two divisions of Monster Trucks,

620
00:43:13,160 --> 00:43:16,080
one that does the exhibitions and the wrestling and the weird stuff,

621
00:43:16,140 --> 00:43:18,780
and maybe we'll be the group that does the real racing,

622
00:43:18,781 --> 00:43:19,680
and I like to see that.

623
00:43:20,660 --> 00:43:21,460
That's my goal.

624
00:43:21,940 --> 00:43:23,900
They're trying to legitimize the sport,

625
00:43:24,000 --> 00:43:25,480
and when you say legitimize the sport,

626
00:43:25,481 --> 00:43:27,960
I mean, we're going to have ballroom dancing in the Olympics,

627
00:43:28,220 --> 00:43:31,340
so who can really say what a legitimate sport is?

628
00:43:32,180 --> 00:43:35,900
It's a little bit of a mix of real sport, legitimate sport,

629
00:43:36,260 --> 00:43:39,540
spectator sport, pro wrestling, NASCAR driving.

630
00:43:42,180 --> 00:43:44,900
Whatever lies in the future, one thing is certain.

631
00:43:45,480 --> 00:43:47,420
Monster trucks won't be going away,

632
00:43:47,840 --> 00:43:49,380
and they won't be getting any smaller.

633
00:43:50,260 --> 00:43:54,420
In the big world of monster trucks, size does matter.

